# submitGuiElementRenderState is NeoForge-ADDED and does not exist in vanilla at all: it is absent
# from the 1.21.6 layered mappings, where every vanilla member is listed by official, intermediary
# and named name, and vanilla GuiGraphics has only a family of specific submitters -- submitBlit,
# submitItem, submitEntityRenderState and so on -- with no generic one.
#
# Vanilla puts the generic route one level down: GuiGraphics holds a GuiRenderState in a private
# field, and GuiRenderState.submitGuiElement takes the element. So the field and that method are
# what get widened, not a method that is not there. Writing an entry for submitGuiElementRenderState
# first cost a round: Loom validates an access widener when it REMAPS THE JAR, after compileJava, so
# Loom accepting an entry is no evidence that the member exists.
#
# This is a second access widener rather than a line in the first one, and the reason is a property
# of the format: an entry naming a member that does not exist on the node being built is a BUILD
# FAILURE, not a no-op. Neither entry here exists below 1.21.6, and the other file's
# RenderType.CompositeState entry is only meaningful below 1.21, so one file cannot serve both.
# The build script picks between them and renames the chosen one to howtogo.accesswidener, which is
# the name fabric.mod.json asks for.
